{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/465188-so-story-tellers","id":465188,"title":"So story tellers","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-04-01T15:04:14-07:00","id":465188,"title":"So story tellers","content":"How much of your stories are detailed? How much of it is improv?<br /> <br /> Do you detail full events for just jot down a couple sentences and see what happens?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> WoD-Dark Eras!! (Backed for Viking Age Werewolf)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1939-lyrics-of-war","name":"Lyrics Of War","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1939_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1769"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-01T15:34:17-07:00","id":465234,"title":"","content":"Broad strokes planned and specifics are improv.<br /> <br /> I generally know where the story is going to go, or at least who's responsible for large scale actions, but how the players eventually end up there or the condition they are in when they make it is flexable.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/785-full-time-gm","name":"Full Time GM","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar785_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"174"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-01T15:51:46-07:00","id":465283,"title":"","content":"SAS like. Story progressions with aims, both for myself and players. Multiple ways to get from one scene to others.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-01T16:31:51-07:00","id":465336,"title":"","content":"I usually come up with an NPC agenda or two that will shake things up, plus a scene to kick off the session. Then I let the players decide how they're going to tackle things. If the game slows down, I throw in an event to ratchet up the pressure.<br /> <br /> I do more world/NPC prep than anything else.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Cavaliers of Mars Creator</b><br /> <b>Retired CofD Lead</b><br /> <br /> Check out my <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Rose%20Bailey\" target=\"_blank\">guides to </a><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/261142/BITE-ME-How-to-Write-Vampire?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> and my <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse/pub/4895/Fantasy-Heartbreaker?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">indie games</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2-rose-bailey","name":"Rose Bailey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2361"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-01T16:56:43-07:00","id":465346,"title":"","content":"I run slice of life type games. I play over Skype via text and pictures rather than video and voicing. Frankly, face to face is too much pressure for me. I present a better story when I can type things out. <br /> <br /> I prep by finding out a lot about the player's interests, their character ideas. <br /> <br /> I generate back story ideas that I think the players will find interesting that will give their characters something to discover, run into, or have find them as things progress. These are broad stroke ideas to start. As the game progresses I try to tighten them up and fit them in. (Or throw them away and make new ones, if it works out that way.) Sometimes I fit them into a larger story idea loosely, or sometimes I just sock them away in files as &quot;ideas&quot;. <br /> <br /> I generate a bunch of interesting NPCs. Broad strokes again - mostly description and story. These I throw in when I need an interesting NPC to show up. <br /> <br /> For some reason I am absolutely in love with apocalypse. Every game I have run has had an undercurrent story of something moving towards some kind of devastating destruction. In my last werewolf game it was the city of New York being swallowed up by spirits of forgotten treasures of the deep and becoming an isolated realm ruled by the Pure on a ruthless, open hunt for mankind.. (Think of all the stories and mythos around sunken ships and their gold, Atlantis, etc..) In a changeling game it was the government accidentally inciting war with Arcadia over their research and 'ventures' into the Hedge. In my current werewolf game it deals with the God Machine, and I'm interested to see it run through. <br /> <br /> Anyway, so I come up with a general &quot;this is moving towards apocalypse&quot; theme to build on. It helps me for moving NPCs and storylines around in the background, especially for generating mysterious &quot;huh?&quot; events. <br /> <br /> Whenever I'm web browsing, if I find an interesting picture of any sort I save it away in a folder for potential inspirational use in a game. A setting, a person, a monster, an item.. etc. I go to these when I need something suddenly on the fly. A picture is worth a thousand words, and that can be a lifesaver in game. <br /> <br /> When the game runs, I swear every time. I have a set of things planned for and it never goes that way. I have to wrestle when ST'ing to get those items covered in the game at all. So inevitably I wind up winging a good amount of it, always trying to keep things interesting and moving in the direction the players desire - while preserving the storylines I've already prepared. I think this really is the art of STing. <br /> <br /> As far as stats and abilities? Almost never. When I need stats and abilities I grab a character example from one of the books and modify it to what I need. At best I will write a page number down to refer to once I get to it in game, or jot some notes about particular skill sets or abilities a given NPC will have. I think I'm a lot looser on that then other people, but I don't have a lot of time during the week to to prepare, so I take shortcuts!<br /> <br /> So wrap up of my overly long post. My prep folder is tons of text files labelled with story lines, characters, and other random ideas. When I improvise something in game, I write it up on the go so that I can remember what I did and try to keep it consistent. <br /> <br /> Oh yeah, and finally if things seem to be going slowly in the game and I'm floundering, I just add something with sex appeal. Works like a charm. <br /> <br /> -going to go prep now.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5220-mother-s-love","name":"Mother's Love","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5220_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"58"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-01T17:00:29-07:00","id":465347,"title":"","content":"I'm fairly new to the GMing scene, but I've found that when I plan too many details in advance it becomes a game of Read-the-GM's-Mind, which is no fun. <br /> A kick off scene and a handful of along-the-way pieces that you can fit in as your characters move around make for a good arsenal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4563-terraben","name":"TerraBen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"278"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-01T17:20:48-07:00","id":465381,"title":"","content":"I plan out my stories like a TV show, with each session being an episode. In session zero we discuss, if this chronicle was a TV show, what genre would it be? What would it be rated? etc. We might draw comparisons to actual shows, or we might use more general terms. After that, I will come up with a rough story arc for about a 12-episode season, but only plan about 6 episodes ahead. For each of these episodes, I might prepare one or two setpiece scenes, but beyond that I just rely on the tropes of the genre and my improvisation chops. Each scene, I ask myself &quot;if this was a [genre] show, what would happen in a scene like this?&quot; and then that thing is what happens. For example, if we're doing a sphagetti western and the players decide they want to go into the saloon, you can bet there's going to be a barroom brawl. At all times, I keep in mind the PCs' goals, both personal and plot-related, and I make it my job to present obstacles to those goals. I present scenarios that pose dramatic questions, but I let the players answer those questions with their actions.<br /> <br /> The first episode is a day in the life of the PCs. Main characters are introduced, and the setpiece scene is some kind of small-scale conflict that foreshadows the overarching conflict of the season. Episode two introduces the overarching conflict - the setpiece scene involves the villain revealing themselves, or the player characters uncovering the conspiracy, or something to that effect. Episodes three and four give the players the opportunity to follow what ever lead they discovered in episode two, or to pursue personal character goals, and the NPC's advance their own agendas accordingly. Episode five is when shit hits the fan; in the setpiece scene, something is going to get blown up, or some important NPC is going to die, or something to that effect. By episode six, there should be some sort of resolution or cliffhanger before the mid-season break. Then I plan out the next 6 episodes, which generally follow the same structure as the previous six, except that episode seven is more of a recap than an introduction, and episode twelve should see the resolution of the season's overarching conflict.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-01T17:22:02-07:00","id":465383,"title":"","content":"I run sandboxes. I do a lot of both.<br /> <br /> I think the first and last games were the most smoothly run. The first because it was a more tightly knit story and player action was pretty easy to predict, the last because with aspirations I was able to really get a handle on what players wanted for their characters.<br /> <br /> Scenes the players go after will have a brief description of the likely mood of involved NPCs and where they are in their script when the players confront them. Then there'll be a few lines for each relevant NPC with things they might want to talk to the PCs about or avoid talking to the PCs about. A while ago I started tacking on lists of things NPCs could offer and things they want to their own descriptions, which helps with a lot of improv, once you get to know the characters.<br /> <br /> When the players stumble upon scenes like at the beginning of sessions there tends to be more detail, as well. Often there will be a few 'If the characters do this or that' reminders for mechanics or other plot developments.<br /> <br /> But the conversations and 'random' encounters are almost entirely improvised except for specific lines I might want to drop. Because of the sandbox environment I've a lot of events that could pop up, and if I think the players are cruising too easily or maybe getting a little disinterested in the run of things I'll let the Phantom of the Monorail appear while they're riding along or a stray from another city run into them.<br /> <br /> Once you really get to know the NPCs, their affectations and their aspirations, the improv really flows from there.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:8px\">The Phantom didn't really appear until much later. He texted while Obfuscated, suggested rather strongly they do not eat from his train and sent picture attachments of them so they would know he was watching them.</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-01T19:21:23-07:00","id":465494,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Rose Bailey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/465188-so-story-tellers?p=465336#post465336\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I usually come up with an NPC agenda or two that will shake things up, plus a scene to kick off the session. Then I let the players decide how they're going to tackle things. If the game slows down, I throw in an event to ratchet up the pressure.<br /> <br /> I do more world/NPC prep than anything else.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's actually exactly how I run my games, too. Unless it's something special - like a convention game or something - wherein I need specific things to happen, or within a time frame. Then I plan more.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>This space for rent.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/552-strider1276","name":"strider1276","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar552_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"382"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-01T23:56:44-07:00","id":465740,"title":"","content":"A lot of it depends on how far in advance of the actual game I can get people's games-or how early I can tell that personal character story isn't going to matter.<br /> <br /> Because of how much character deconstruction goes into my story structure, the first few games tend to be improv until I can get my feet under me for larger ideas.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T00:07:48-07:00","id":465748,"title":"","content":"I run games via Skype (much like Mother's Love, I have a harder time in face-to-face games due to a rather nasty first encounter the hobby) and while Skype's much slower than tabletop it offers me a greater ability to enrich the setting. I tend to think of broad themes first and then as the players make their characters, I see what they're building around and mesh those in with my plot. I kick off usually with in media res or with an immediate &quot;this is the horrible thing that forges you lot into a group&quot; revelation and drop pieces of the overarching story as I tend to go. I'm terrible at sandbox, so my games are narrative run with player actions driving the story itself.<br /> <br /> As for actual sessions, I plan for one session ahead with some focus, but everything's left in broad strokes, scene snippets/ideas, and a running excel sheet of what the NPCs are doing when the players aren't messing with their plans. I've found that if I plan any further, I'll back myself into corners or become too worried about how to fit the current session into the one down the line.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/606-aynie","name":"Aynie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar606_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T00:15:32-07:00","id":465756,"title":"","content":"I aim for an SAS style on mine. Each scene gets a page, maybe two if it's very important. Each page details the major plot points, potential actions and consequences and any stats for enemies/npcs. I don't plan every little touch because more often than not my group is going to take things in in a slightly different direction. I end up improvising about 20% of any particular story.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1824-xaielao","name":"Xaielao","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1824_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"94"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T03:49:45-07:00","id":465902,"title":"","content":"I too do it Bailey and Fisher style. I have clearly defined NPCs with their own Aspirations and I jumpstart the chronicle with an event that desestabilizes things. I tend to have one big plot and at least one personal sideplot for each character, of course that's manageable because I only have three players, in bigger games that would be too much.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T04:03:00-07:00","id":465905,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Xaielao</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/465188-so-story-tellers?p=465756#post465756\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I aim for an SAS style on mine. Each scene gets a page, maybe two if it's very important. Each page details the major plot points, potential actions and consequences and any stats for enemies/npcs. I don't plan every little touch because more often than not my group is going to take things in in a slightly different direction. I end up improvising about 20% of any particular story.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Hey, what does SAS style mean exactly?<br /> <br /> I love hearing about other ST's methods and opinions, thanks everyone for sharing. I had been thinking about posting a question about pacing time as a related ST question.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5220-mother-s-love","name":"Mother's Love","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5220_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"58"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T04:09:08-07:00","id":465909,"title":"","content":"I usually start with thinking about major story beats*. The beginning, some major dramatic events in the middle and roughly where it is supposed to go (pending player actions). That usually helps to fill out the space between those things.<br /> <br /> Generally, I have about a sentence each for important scenes I think are coming up (to remind me what really matters). When I *know* a player is going to do something (because they said so or have an Aspiration to that effect) I usually spend a bit more time on the details. For example, in my VtR game I have prepared a couple of &quot;Touchstone Scenes&quot; ahead of time, so when a player visits their Touchstone I have ideas for interesting scenarios to happen.<br /> <br /> *: Indeed, many of my Chronicle ideas were spawned from thinking about a cool climactic scene.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Politeness is the lubricant of social intercourse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/272-saibot","name":"saibot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar272_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1063"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T04:09:25-07:00","id":465910,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/465188-so-story-tellers?p=465381#post465381\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I plan out my stories like a TV show, with each session being an episode. In session zero we discuss, if this chronicle was a TV show, what genre would it be? What would it be rated? etc. We might draw comparisons to actual shows, or we might use more general terms. After that, I will come up with a rough story arc for about a 12-episode season, but only plan about 6 episodes ahead. For each of these episodes, I might prepare one or two setpiece scenes, but beyond that I just rely on the tropes of the genre and my improvisation chops. Each scene, I ask myself &quot;if this was a [genre] show, what would happen in a scene like this?&quot; and then that thing is what happens. For example, if we're doing a sphagetti western and the players decide they want to go into the saloon, you can bet there's going to be a barroom brawl. At all times, I keep in mind the PCs' goals, both personal and plot-related, and I make it my job to present obstacles to those goals. I present scenarios that pose dramatic questions, but I let the players answer those questions with their actions.<br /> <br /> The first episode is a day in the life of the PCs. Main characters are introduced, and the setpiece scene is some kind of small-scale conflict that foreshadows the overarching conflict of the season. Episode two introduces the overarching conflict - the setpiece scene involves the villain revealing themselves, or the player characters uncovering the conspiracy, or something to that effect. Episodes three and four give the players the opportunity to follow what ever lead they discovered in episode two, or to pursue personal character goals, and the NPC's advance their own agendas accordingly. Episode five is when shit hits the fan; in the setpiece scene, something is going to get blown up, or some important NPC is going to die, or something to that effect. By episode six, there should be some sort of resolution or cliffhanger before the mid-season break. Then I plan out the next 6 episodes, which generally follow the same structure as the previous six, except that episode seven is more of a recap than an introduction, and episode twelve should see the resolution of the season's overarching conflict.</div> </div> </div> </div>Do your games run that organized? I am amazed. I love this idea of following TV show themes, it makes a lot of sense for good storytelling. <br /> <br /> But STing players is like herding cats - how do you manage to stick to the season schedule??<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5220-mother-s-love","name":"Mother's Love","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5220_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"58"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T06:59:17-07:00","id":466022,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mother's Love</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/465188-so-story-tellers?p=465910#post465910\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Do your games run that organized? I am amazed. I love this idea of following TV show themes, it makes a lot of sense for good storytelling. <br /> <br /> But STing players is like herding cats - how do you manage to stick to the season schedule??</div> </div> </div> </div>Haha no. Like I said, I basically just plan a few setpiece scenes that move the plot along, and the rest is just improvisation and genre tropes. Mostly what I'm doing is planting plot hooks to give the players goals, and paying attention to their personal character goals, and throwing obstacles in the way of those goals. I'm responding to their actions in ways that are appropriate to the genre. For the sepiece scenes, I'm flexible about when and where they happen. If the players are following a lead on a plot hook, that's a signal they're itching for the overarching story to move forward, so I have what ever they're doing lead them to that scene. Kind of SAS style, that episode guide is like a list of the key scenes in an SAS game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T07:18:18-07:00","id":466031,"title":"","content":"While I like the sas style I prefer trying for something more sandbox, giving the players that mmo style freedom while sticking to the plot. <br /> <br /> I do find that depends on the player though and their ability to think on their feet. Some people struggle with that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> WoD-Dark Eras!! (Backed for Viking Age Werewolf)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1939-lyrics-of-war","name":"Lyrics Of War","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1939_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1769"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T07:30:54-07:00","id":466038,"title":"","content":"Yeah I would love to run a sandbox game, but I've tried it a few times and my players have never been able to get into it. I don't know if it's them who need more structure to know what to do or me who just isn't good at running sandboox style games. Probably a mix of both, but either way that style just doesn't work for my regular group.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T07:51:37-07:00","id":466081,"title":"","content":"Set the backdrop and give it some details; hammer out the main factions, movers and shakers and set their agendas into motion, then drop the PCs into the stew and watch everything explode catastrophically and in hilariously unpredictable ways.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>- Chris Allen, Aberrant Line Developer, Freelance Writer<br /> <br /> \u200bLike my work? Feel like helping me stay supplied with tea? Check out my <b><a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/acrozatarim\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1757-acrozatarim","name":"Acrozatarim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T08:02:11-07:00","id":466094,"title":"","content":"This has been a great thread so far. I hope a lot more people contribute to it.<br /> <br /> I've noticed two recurring jargon terms that I'd like to get nailed down. Here's my best stab at them:<br /> <br /> SAS: As far as I know, that's the Storyteller Adventure System acronym. While I've not read anything explicitly spelling out how it goes, I did play all the one-offs last summer (the Werewolf thing with the glass spirits at the gas station, the Promethean game where everyone spends half of it strapped to a table, the Geist story mirroring Son of Sam, etc), which I think were built in SAS format. If that's the case, they all had a complied list of scenes and a flow chart showing how they would connect based on character decisions. Each scene had a page or two write up with goals for both the characters and NPCs, as well as blurbs about the NPCs and any major rolls that might need to take place during the scene.<br /> Is that more or less the whole of SAS?<br /> <br /> Sandbox: This seems to be a catch-all term for &quot;I didn't write a plot because I want the players to do their own thing&quot;. Writing it out like that sounds kinda derogatory. I didn't mean it to be and I'm sorry to anyone who uses this style that took it that way. Moreso, I just mean you as a Storyteller built a setting wherein some stuff is going to happen, but you have no intention of forcing a plot. The characters can do as they see fit in the space and grow as they'd like.<br /> Is that what's meant?<br /> <br /> <br /> Edit: One other thing - when running a game over skype, are you doing a multi-channel video call, or just a voice thing? I've been in games where the whole thing is done via collaboratively writing a google doc, (which is slower in the ways the Skype people have described), but I've not used Skype that way ever.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4563,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4563-terraben","user":"TerraBen","date":". 04-02-2015, 11:06 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4563-terraben","name":"TerraBen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"278"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T08:16:05-07:00","id":466128,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TerraBen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/465188-so-story-tellers?p=466094#post466094\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Edit: One other thing - when running a game over skype, are you doing a multi-channel video call, or just a voice thing? I've been in games where the whole thing is done via collaboratively writing a google doc, (which is slower in the ways the Skype people have described), but I've not used Skype that way ever.</div> </div> </div> </div>Regardless of the group, when I run games on Skype, it's done via text (so, one of the upsides to the game is a ton of description and introspective - and I can run as many scenes simultaneously as I can handle/the story dictates. The downside to text is when you've got 4+ people all writing extremely well, it can be a long time between posts. If I'm gaming with good friends, occasionally we'll open the OOC chat to voice as well as handling everything via text.<br /> <br /> I've never run a game over voice, though. It runs into the same issues as table-top for me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/606-aynie","name":"Aynie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar606_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T08:24:01-07:00","id":466134,"title":"","content":"That's essentially accurate. SAS, or Storytelling Adventure System, is the format most pre-packaged WoD adventures are written in. It involves a setting, a cast of NPCs with goals and motivations, and a selection of scenes, which are flexible in what order they occur in (and some may or may not even occur). Flowchart are used to give the ST a guideline on how the scenes might play out based on the players' decisions, a bit like a chose your own adventure story, but the ST also has some responsibility to go outside of those guidelines as the need arises.<br /> <br /> Sandbox games are your open-world games; the Skyrims and GTAs of pen and paper roleplaying. They don't have to be entirely without a plot, but if there is a plot, it's often completely optional. However, a key feature of sandbox games is that the world is &quot;alive&quot;. If the players ignore the apocalyptic doom dragon to go pick flowers, he's going to fuck shit up for the world whether they feel like doing the main quest right now or not.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T08:34:14-07:00","id":466144,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/465188-so-story-tellers?p=466134#post466134\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If the players ignore the apocalyptic doom dragon to go pick flowers, he's going to fuck shit up for the world whether they feel like doing the main quest right now or not.</div> </div> </div> </div>That would make sense, though the freedom to go pick flowers and ignore the apocalyptic doom dragaon for however long is one of the nicest facets of Skyrim, in my oppinion. Is that the aspect of sandboxes that makes it hard for you and your groups?<br /> Thanks for the clarifications!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4563-terraben","name":"TerraBen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"278"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T08:42:25-07:00","id":466151,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/465188-so-story-tellers?p=466134#post466134\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sandbox games are your open-world games; the Skyrims and GTAs of pen and paper roleplaying. They don't have to be entirely without a plot, but if there is a plot, it's often completely optional.</div> </div> </div> </div>I prefer Saint's Row as an analogy. Silly games in some places, but very well designed. The first ones especially managed to tie your instincts as a game-player, namely, 'advancing in power,' to tie together with the story. While an active plotline can be completely optional, I try to tie the 'main' plot directly to the PC goals. The first Saint's Row games also manage to do dynamic events well, events throughout the city are scripted and will occur if you're in the area, whether you're looking or not, and expanding the world in response to player action. Though those last two are mainly something I like due to my history with MUDs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 04-02-2015, 11:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T09:22:21-07:00","id":466209,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TerraBen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/465188-so-story-tellers?p=466144#post466144\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That would make sense, though the freedom to go pick flowers and ignore the apocalyptic doom dragaon for however long is one of the nicest facets of Skyrim, in my oppinion. Is that the aspect of sandboxes that makes it hard for you and your groups?<br /> Thanks for the clarifications!</div> </div> </div> </div>No problem! For my group, the players often just don't know what to do with themselves in a sandbox game. Additionally, they tend to lose interest if they don't feel like they're advancing towards something. We can't manage long-running chronicles for the same reason, they need a structure with a pre-determined beginning, middle, and end to feel like they're accomplishing something. That's probably at least partially my own fault for not making their characters' impact on the world obvious enough, or not giving them distinct enough objectives. But it's also just the way my group is, they're more interested in accomplishing tasks than exploration.<br /> <br /> I am going to attempt another sandbox game in the near future with just one player, we'll see how that goes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T12:50:20-07:00","id":466408,"title":"","content":"I run sandbox settings. I detail out the area and the NPCs. My plots are usually based on NPCs pursuing agendas. I keep a page in my game wiki with which plots are active, on deck, resolved, or back-burnered. Generally, one to three plots are active at a time. One or two are on deck, waiting for an active plot to get resolved or back-burnered, or to be triggered by another plot ending or some prerequisite to happen. Back-burnered plots (there are usually two or three) become active again when something triggers them, often just as the PCs forget about the dangling thread. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> This may make it sound like it is very planned, but it isn't. When I come up with plots, it is usually just a couple of sentences with a vague premise. I toss that out and then improv based on the NPCs' personalities and agendas. The plot tracking is mostly just record-keeping to keep track of what's happening in a simple format. It also keeps me from having the NPCs be purely reactive, which I find is a common trap in sandbox settings. With the plot tracking, I can keep track of what the NPCs are doing and check it before each session to figure out their next steps. Having multiple plots going not only allows for fun resource conflicts (which fire do we put out?) but also lets the world seem to be deeper and more complex, particularly when the plots interweave.<br /> <br /> (Sorry if this is a bit scattered. Writing the post around software upgrade steps.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Eric Christian Berg</b><br /> <i>Onyx Path Freelancer</i><br /> Promethean: The Created Second Edition, Dark Eras Companion","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2019-eberg","name":"eberg","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2019_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"284"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T13:09:17-07:00","id":466426,"title":"","content":"We run it as a tv-soap basically. Main focus on wolf blooded point of view. Low combat, more story and interaction stuff.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/860-ephraim","name":"Ephraim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar860_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"70"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T13:32:41-07:00","id":466448,"title":"","content":"To slightly expand on my previous point, there are <i>some</i> plots and such that I write.<br /> <br /> All of my WoD games take place in the same universe/continuity. As such, I have some big plots wandering about in the background, some major NPC movers-and-shakers, and so on. Those all need more long-term planning than the characters I'm using in my next <b>Requiem</b> session, for instance.<br /> <br /> Also, everything, from almost every book, can be found somewhere in the world, even if it's a very small portion. Whether or not any PC's interact with them or even know they exist? That's up to the story, and what happens as a result.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>This space for rent.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/552-strider1276","name":"strider1276","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar552_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"382"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T14:59:48-07:00","id":466552,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/465188-so-story-tellers?p=466209#post466209\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am going to attempt another sandbox game in the near future with just one player, we'll see how that goes.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hope it goes well - I have never played a short game that felt satisfactorily &quot;finished&quot;. Long term games have much more satisfying character development as you don't feel pressured to fit too much in a short time, or fill up a long time. It doesn't feel too much like a waste if a couple characters sit around and fight or chat or flirt or do whatever they want to do for a session. It also feels pretty cool to say at different points, &quot;let's just skip ahead a year at this point&quot; and span a long period of time in a character's existence. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5220-mother-s-love","name":"Mother's Love","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5220_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"58"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T15:24:11-07:00","id":466572,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mother's Love</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/465188-so-story-tellers?p=466552#post466552\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> It also feels pretty cool to say at different points, &quot;let's just skip ahead a year at this point&quot; and span a long period of time in a character's existence.</div> </div> </div> </div>We did something like that in my last vampire game. It really made me wish I had chosen a historical jumping off point for the game, instead of starting two years before the, then, current year. Except my players have a palpable lack of interest when it comes to history, like a gnashing void of non-existent teeth rendering my suggestions to nullity. I'm not any expert or anything but there's a lot of cool out of the way areas that would have been perfect. And now you're not even limited to doing it with vampire, with the new Mummy and I think Demons are potentially timeless.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-02T17:08:02-07:00","id":466678,"title":"","content":"Well good thing there's the new dark eras timelines!!! It's funny though that the werewolf timelines added are things I could have done anyway with my studies. <br /> <br /> I think the best way to get players involved in the sandbox would be to utilize some of the stuff from the story teller section of 2e core. Ask them what they see, or what's happening. If they see a carnival then roll with that. It's something I'm gonna be trying with my wife to see if it gets her more involved.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> WoD-Dark Eras!! (Backed for Viking Age Werewolf)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1939-lyrics-of-war","name":"Lyrics Of War","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1939_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1769"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-05T03:44:51-07:00","id":469276,"title":"","content":"All I tend to run are sandbox games. I make a list of themes that I want to explore, get input from the players on what they want and use that to highlight specific themes. Then I createas many NPCs as I can think of, even if they're as generic as &quot;smooth talking conartist with vampire ties&quot;. I make a broad sketch of the city with a few specific places detailed (I also offer bonus xp to players who create their own weird/interesting places). Finally, I make an outline of what's going on in the city and where the setting will end up if the players do *nothing* as well as plot seeds that lead into these setting changing events.<br /> <br /> What this does is have the setting grow and move around the players no matter if they follow up on the story seeds or if they ignore them. All those NPCs can be plugged into whatever plot the players are pursuing, even if it's a personal storyline. The elodath looking for the father that vanished one night may run into that smooth talking conman with vampire ties which can guide his personal storyline into a bigger, setting-wide one.<br /> <br /> I find that this gives the players agency enough to feel like their choices matter (which they should) while still keeping the game managable or frustrating me by writing out half a dozen storylines that never see play. You could adapt this for more focused playstyles, too. The trick with sandboxes, is making sure the players know that they're going to be responsible for moving things forward, otherwise the world will pass them by.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5337-soonmot","name":"soonmot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5337_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"64"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-08T08:18:51-07:00","id":472790,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>soonmot</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/465188-so-story-tellers?p=469276#post469276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I also offer bonus xp to players who create their own weird/interesting places.</div> </div> </div> </div>That was really helpful. I'm curious how you handle the quoted bit with the Beats system. Do you just go &quot;That's awesome, take a beat&quot; when the player suggests they go to a place and describes it well?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4563-terraben","name":"TerraBen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"278"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}